
1437 Problem :-

Given an binary array nums and an integer k, return true if all 1's are at least k places away from each other, otherwise return false.

Example 1:
Input: nums = [1,0,0,0,1,0,0,1], k = 2
Output: true
Explanation: Each of the 1s are at least 2 places away from each other.

Example 2:
Input: nums = [1,0,0,1,0,1], k = 2
Output: false
Explanation: The second 1 and third 1 are only one apart from each other.
 
Constraints:

1 <= nums.length <= 105
0 <= k <= nums.length
nums[i] is 0 or 1

----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Solution :-

Intuition
Two 1s must be separated by at least k zeros.
That means:
number of elements between them (i - prev - 1) >= k
So the intuition is:
Keep track of the last position where a 1 was found.
Every time a new 1 appears, check if the gap satisfies the requirement.
If the gap is too small, the condition fails.
This is simply a single pass comparison of distances between consecutive 1s.

Approach
The function checks whether all 1s in the binary array nums are at least k places apart.
To do this, it:
Scans the array from left to right.
Records the index of the previous 1 found.
For each new 1 encountered, it computes the distance (i − previous_index − 1).
If the distance is less than k, it returns false.
Otherwise, it updates previous_index and continues scanning.

Complexity
Time complexity:
O(n)

Space complexity:
O(1)

Code :-
class Solution {
    public boolean kLengthApart(int[] nums, int k) {
        int lastIndex = -1;

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.length; i++) {

            if (nums[i] == 1) {
                if (lastIndex != -1 && i - lastIndex - 1 < k) {
                    return false;
                }
                lastIndex = i;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
}
